# The node foundation

Every node in the Loom AST — a container like a section or a leaf like a token — shares one shape and is built by one combinator. `LoomNode` is that foundation: it defines source positions, the health and diagnostic vocabulary every node carries, `loomNode`, which stamps the shared `type`, `position`, `source`, and `health` fields onto each node schema, and `nodeOf`, which constructs a node of a schema.

## Built on Effect Schema

Every node type is an Effect `Schema.Struct`, so one definition both validates a node and gives it a type.

=>

import { Option, Schema } from 'effect'

## Source positions

A `Position` is the span a node covers — a `start` and `end` `Point`. A `Point` carries a `line`, an optional `column`, and an `offset`. The `offset` is the byte index Volar maps against; line and column only serve diagnostics.

`shifted` moves a point along its line by a count of characters, forward or back, so a reader of a token can name a span inside it, such as one name of an anchor's path.

=>

const intAtLeast = (min: number) =>
  Schema.makeFilter<number>((n) =>
    Number.isInteger(n) && n >= min ? undefined : `must be an integer >= ${min}`,
  )

export const PointSchema = Schema.Struct({
  line: Schema.Number.check(intAtLeast(1)),
  column: Schema.optional(Schema.Number.check(intAtLeast(1))),
  offset: Schema.Number.check(intAtLeast(0)),
})
export type Point = typeof PointSchema.Type

export const PositionSchema = Schema.Struct({
  start: PointSchema,
  end: PointSchema,
})
export type Position = typeof PositionSchema.Type

export const shifted = (point: Point, by: number): Point =>
  Option.match(Option.fromNullishOr(point.column), {
    onNone: () => ({ ...point, offset: point.offset + by }),
    onSome: (column) => ({ ...point, offset: point.offset + by, column: column + by }),
  })

## Diagnostics

A `Diagnostic` is a positioned message with a `Severity` — `error`, `warning`, or `info`. `info` is not a failure: a node carrying only `info` diagnostics stays healthy.

A diagnostic may also carry `fixes`, the edits an editor offers to repair what it reports. A `Fix` holds the `title` the editor shows and the `text` it writes. The text replaces the span the diagnostic covers, or the span `at` names when the fix edits somewhere else. A fix that names a `file` writes a new loom there instead, whose whole text is the fix's text, as a box that points to a chapter nobody has written offers to write it. An ambiguous anchor offers one fix for each path that names one of its candidates alone, and each fix writes that path in place of the anchor's name. An anchor that names no section offers a fix that declares the section: a heading, written at the point below the section that holds the anchor.

=>

export const SeveritySchema = Schema.Literals(['error', 'warning', 'info'])
export type Severity = typeof SeveritySchema.Type

export const FixSchema = Schema.Struct({
  title: Schema.String,
  text: Schema.String,
  at: Schema.optional(PositionSchema),
  file: Schema.optional(Schema.String),
})
export type Fix = typeof FixSchema.Type

export const DiagnosticSchema = Schema.Struct({
  message: Schema.String,
  position: PositionSchema,
  severity: SeveritySchema,
  fixes: Schema.optional(Schema.Array(FixSchema)),
})
export type Diagnostic = typeof DiagnosticSchema.Type

## Node health

Every node carries `Health`: a `status` and the diagnostics behind it. `ok` is final and clean; `error` and `warning` describe problems; `incomplete` marks a node an earlier pass left for a later one to finish. `okHealth` and `incompleteHealth` are the shared constants for the two cases with no diagnostics.

=>

export const HealthStatusSchema = Schema.Literals(['ok', 'error', 'warning', 'incomplete'])
export type HealthStatus = typeof HealthStatusSchema.Type

export const HealthSchema = Schema.Struct({
  status: HealthStatusSchema,
  diagnostics: Schema.Array(DiagnosticSchema),
})
export type Health = typeof HealthSchema.Type

export const okHealth: Health = { status: 'ok', diagnostics: [] }

export const incompleteHealth: Health = {
  status: 'incomplete',
  diagnostics: [],
}

## Rejected fragments

An `UnexpectedToken` is a stray fragment the schema rejected — an orphan bracket, a duplicate tag — kept with its position and text rather than dropped. It carries no health of its own: its presence in a node's `unexpected` array is the anomaly, and the parent node's health reflects it.

=>

export const UnexpectedTokenSchema = Schema.Struct({
  type: Schema.tag('UnexpectedToken'),
  position: PositionSchema,
  value: Schema.String,
})
export type UnexpectedToken = typeof UnexpectedTokenSchema.Type

## The node combinator

`loomNode` builds a node schema from a tag and the node's own fields. To every node it adds the shared four — a `type` literal that discriminates the union, the `position`, the `source` slice the node covers, and its `health` — plus an optional `unexpected` array. A walker then recognises any node by its `type` and recurses into any field that has one.

=>

export const loomNode = <Tag extends string, Fields extends Schema.Struct.Fields>(
  tag: Tag,
  fields: Fields,
) =>
  Schema.Struct({
    type: Schema.tag(tag),
    position: PositionSchema,
    source: Schema.String,
    health: HealthSchema,
    unexpected: Schema.optional(Schema.Array(UnexpectedTokenSchema)),
    ...fields,
  })

## Constructing a node

Loom constructs a node for every token and every line it reads. A chapter of a few thousand lines makes tens of thousands of nodes, and the editor makes them again on every keystroke.

A schema can construct its own value, through its `make` method. That method walks the whole schema on every call, to fill in defaults and to run checks, and the walk costs about ten times what the node itself costs to construct. For a node the walk adds one thing only, the `type` tag. The compiler has already checked every field the caller wrote.

`nodeOf` constructs a node without the walk. It takes the node's schema and the node's fields without the tag. It reads the tag the schema declares, and gives back the fields with that tag. The schema still names the node at the place where the node is constructed, and the compiler still checks every field against the schema's type. A `NodeSchema` is any schema `nodeOf` can read a tag from: a schema `loomNode` built, or any other struct whose `type` field is a `Schema.tag`.

`nodeOf` checks nothing while the program runs, so it suits a value the program itself makes. A value that arrives from outside the program, such as a manifest or a configuration file, is decoded by its schema instead. So is a value whose fit is the very question: the tokeniser decodes a label to learn whether it is well formed.

=>

type Tagged = { readonly type: string }

export type NodeSchema<Node extends Tagged> = {
  readonly Type: Node
  readonly fields: { readonly type: { readonly schema: { readonly literal: Node['type'] } } }
}

export const nodeOf = <Node extends Tagged>(
  schema: NodeSchema<Node>,
  fields: Omit<Node, 'type'>,
): Node => ({ ...fields, type: schema.fields.type.schema.literal }) as Node

## Telling nodes apart

Most of what Loom does to a node begins with one question: which kind of node is this? The reader asks it of every line, the tokeniser of every weft, and the projection of every part of a section's code.

A matcher answers the question by trying its cases in turn until one fits. A matcher of nine cases takes about 130 nanoseconds to answer, and Loom asks tens of thousands of times for one chapter, and again on every keystroke. A table answers by reading one property. Its cases are a record keyed by the tag, and a call takes the case that stands under the node's own tag, in about 20 nanoseconds.

`byType` builds such a table for a union of nodes. It takes one case for every tag of the union, as the `Cases` type says. The compiler refuses a table that lacks a tag, that names a tag the union does not hold, or whose case takes a node other than the node of its tag. `byType` gives back a function of a node, which calls the node's case. A table is built once, when its module loads, and called wherever a node arrives. A `Data.TaggedEnum` carries the same table under the name `$match`, keyed by `_tag`, and `byType` is that table for Loom's nodes, which are tagged by `type`.

Some cases need more than the node. The second type argument names the further arguments, and a call passes them after the node. A table built with `byType<Part, [home: Location]>()` is called as `table(part, home)`, and each case receives the part and then the home. A case names only the arguments it reads.

`byTypeOr` builds a table for some of the tags, with a default that answers for every other. It finds a case by the table's own keys alone, so a tag that happens to match a name every object inherits, such as `constructor`, falls to the default.

=>

export type Cases<Node extends Tagged, Args extends ReadonlyArray<unknown>, Result> = {
  readonly [Tag in Node['type']]: (
    node: Extract<Node, { readonly type: Tag }>,
    ...args: Args
  ) => Result
}

export const byType =
  <Node extends Tagged, Args extends ReadonlyArray<unknown> = []>() =>
  <Result>(cases: Cases<Node, Args, Result>) =>
  (node: Node, ...args: Args): Result =>
    (cases[node.type as Node['type']] as (node: Node, ...args: Args) => Result)(node, ...args)

export const byTypeOr =
  <Node extends Tagged, Args extends ReadonlyArray<unknown> = []>() =>
  <Result>(
    cases: Partial<Cases<Node, Args, Result>>,
    otherwise: (node: Node, ...args: Args) => Result,
  ) =>
  (node: Node, ...args: Args): Result =>
    (Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(cases, node.type)
      ? (cases[node.type as Node['type']] as (node: Node, ...args: Args) => Result)
      : otherwise)(node, ...args)

